            <h1 class="page-title">Character networks</h1>
            <h2>Overview</h2>
            <p>Character networks visualize the relationship among characters in a novel.
                These visualizations demonstrate how different constellations of characters are
                interconnected in braided narratives.</p>
            <h2>How to read</h2>
          <p>Social network graphs are comprised of nodes (circles) and edges (lines connecting the nodes). In our study, nodes represent characters, and edges represent relationships between two characters.
          The size of the node represents its weighted degree, which measures how many total edges connect to that node. Edges are thicker if two characters have multiple relationships over the course of the novel.</p> 
          
          <p>For each novel, we created three visualizations based on the type of edges. In the Story Space Networks, edges represent relationships in the fictional world of the novel: two characters know each other or know of each other. We developed this method to best representing the diversity of the storyworld. In the Text Space Networks, edges link characters who appear on the same page whether or not they actually have a relationship in the storyworld. This method builds on the work of Graham Alexander Sack (2014)<sup>1</sup> and Martin Grandjean (2013)<sup>2</sup>. In the Exchanges Only Networks, the edges represent acts of communication between characters such as dialogue, reported speech, etc. For all of the networks, we only recorded one relationship between two characters per page, so if a dialogue extends for one page, it is one exchange; if it extends for two pages, it is two exchanges. This method follows the work of Sam Alexander (2019)<sup>3</sup> and Franco Moretti (2011)<sup>4</sup>. We believe that the Story Space method best represents the social networks of the storyworld. Analyzing the Story Space Networks in tandem with the Text Space networks may help extend Alex Woloch’s work on character space.<sup>5</sup> The Exchanges Only method does not work as well for novels as it does for plays.</p>
          

